Distinguish temperature equilibrium and a thermometer indication (@physics-temperature-evidence: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Equilibrium-state-variables-and-local-description.lingua#r_7ZE016Z66H53QB69W5F3HVEN5X") { r_0A30014C7B440AC09EF7087493
Two systems in thermal equilibrium have no sustained net heat transfer between them when the relevant thermal contact is allowed and other influences are controlled. Microscopic energy exchanges need not stop; their macroscopic balance is the point. The zeroth-law relation connects temperature comparisons: systems separately in thermal equilibrium with the same reference are in thermal equilibrium with one another under the corresponding conditions. This permits a calibrated thermometric property to serve as a comparison, rather than identifying temperature with a person's sensation of warmth.

The kelvin is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. Its current definition fixes the Boltzmann constant’s numerical value at 1.380649×10⁻²³ J/K. Read the official definition and realization distinction through [Practical realizations of SI units — BIPM consultative committees](../../inquiry/metrology/sources/SI-Realizations.lingua#r_240WHEHDVW9TR241AXP6T4STM0). Fixing a constant does not make a particular thermometer reading exact. A practical scale, a calibration, sensor self-heating, response time, thermal contact, gradients and the quantity being targeted still matter. The full kelvin-realization and measurement courses remain later preparation.

The shared [Absolute temperature, units and reference levels](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Absolute-temperature-units-and-reference-levels.lingua#r_36PSQZ15KGX9RBTGGBV1G7Q06S) lesson gives the offset relation between Celsius and kelvin. For the later teaching states, 300 K corresponds to 26.85 °C and 600 K to 326.85 °C. The absolute-temperature ratio is two; the ratio of the Celsius numbers is not. Both scales give a 300-degree-sized difference. An equation involving a temperature difference and one involving an absolute-temperature ratio therefore demand different care. Likewise, a pressure gauge's reference must be restored before using an absolute-pressure equation of state.

A thermometer interacts with what it measures. If a sensor begins at a different temperature, equilibration can change the sample as well as the sensor. An observed stable reading can instead reflect a steady balance of several heat exchanges. Describe the quantity as, for example, a specified region's temperature over a stated observation interval, rather than treating every point in a vessel as equal because one sensor stopped changing. Reuse [Begin with a question and specify the measurand](../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Question-and-measurand.lingua#r_7ZNCJFEGXNJSGJJJKGYH6MGFE6), [Separate observed variation from uncertainty about a result](../../inquiry/core/Separate-observed-variation-from-uncertainty-about-a-result.lingua#r_0A1A2CA3D01039474C1F5678C7) and the shared metrology temperature lesson by context when its detailed response model is needed.

The next microscopic lesson will derive a relation between translational kinetic energy and temperature for a classical equilibrium ideal gas. That restricted result is not a universal definition saying every material's total internal energy is proportional to temperature. Interaction energy, internal molecular modes and quantum behavior require additional models. A finite velocity table with some matching averages does not by itself prove that the sample is in thermal equilibrium.

**Read and investigate on paper:** [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), sections 1.1–1.2, with the current SI qualification in its source note; [Practical realizations of SI units — BIPM consultative committees](../../inquiry/metrology/sources/SI-Realizations.lingua#r_240WHEHDVW9TR241AXP6T4STM0) for the defining constant. Draw two candidate explanations for a stable sensor indication: a uniformly equilibrated sample and a nonuniform system with balanced heat flows. Specify an additional observation that could distinguish them. Explain why sensor agreement, calibration and an equilibrium assumption answer different questions. No actual thermometry experiment is claimed by this exercise.

**Continue:** [Equilibrium, state variables and local description](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Equilibrium-state-variables-and-local-description.lingua#r_7ZE016Z66H53QB69W5F3HVEN5X).

